# 01. Frozen Context Inherited from Phases 01–03

## 01.1 Role model

| Role | Responsibility | Experience identity |
|---|---|---|
| Student | BUILD | Build projects, learn, execute, understand progress |
| Mentor | SUPERVISE | Guide students, supervise projects, intervene |
| Admin | GOVERN | Govern platform, inspect health, AI, security, cost |

The three role names are conceptual/product language. They do not imply that every account automatically possesses every role.

Authorization is backend-authoritative.

---

## 01.2 Canonical project lifecycle

```text
IDEA
  ↓
ASSESSMENT
  ↓
BLUEPRINT
  ↓
PLANNING
  ↓
IMPLEMENTATION
  ↓
TESTING
  ↓
DEPLOYMENT
  ↓
COMPLETED
```

The frontend represents this lifecycle but does not become its source of truth.

---

## 01.3 Canonical project information chain

```text
Task
  ↓
Milestone
  ↓
Progress
  ↓
Phase
  ↓
Health
```

This is a reusable frontend information model.

A task contributes to milestone progress. Milestones contribute to project progress. Project progress is contextualized by the current lifecycle phase and health signals.

The frontend must not independently invent conflicting progress calculations when canonical backend values exist.

---

## 01.4 Project Definition vs Student Project Instance

These are separate concepts.

### Project Definition

A reusable mentor-authored project template/specification.

### Student Project Instance

A student's actual project created from or associated with a definition.

Changing a Project Definition does not silently mutate an existing Student Project Instance.

Existing students may receive a notification and may independently adopt a proposed change through the Project Change workflow.

This separation must remain visible in:

- routes;
- labels;
- permissions;
- UI copy;
- API boundaries;
- audit events;
- change workflows.

---

## 01.5 AI architecture

The frontend never calls an AI provider directly.

Canonical flow:

```text
Frontend
   ↓
FastAPI
   ↓
Application / Generation Service
   ↓
LangGraph
   ↓
Agents + Tools
   ↓
Structured Output
   ↓
Validation
   ↓
QA / Judge
   ↓
Deterministic Persistence
   ↓
Document Rendering
   ↓
Event
   ↓
Frontend
```

Exactly 12 logical AI agents are part of the current architecture:

1. Idea
2. Scope
3. Technology
4. Features
5. Specification
6. MVP
7. Timeline / Duration
8. Risk
9. Task
10. Milestone
11. README
12. QA / Judge

The frontend treats these as execution/domain concepts and does not expose provider-specific implementation details unless an authorized Admin observability surface explicitly requires them.

---

## 01.6 AI Provider Gateway

All AI provider access goes through the central AI Provider Gateway.

No:

- frontend provider SDK;
- agent-specific provider credential;
- browser-side API key;
- direct provider URL;
- hard-coded provider-specific frontend workflow

is permitted.

---

## 01.7 RAG

RAG is:

- project-scoped;
- derived/rebuildable;
- separate from canonical PostgreSQL state.

The frontend can display RAG/document-processing status but must not treat the RAG index as the canonical project database.

---

## 01.8 Background jobs and events

Long-running work is persistent.

A browser disconnect must not terminate:

- blueprint generation;
- document generation;
- RAG processing;
- long-running AI workflows;
- other persistent background jobs.

SSE is a delivery mechanism, not the job execution mechanism.

The frontend must reconcile state after reconnecting.

