# 49. Frontend Implementation Rules

## Rule 01 — Backend is authoritative

Never treat client state as canonical domain state.

## Rule 02 — No silent architecture changes

Do not alter frozen architecture without explicit phase review.

## Rule 03 — One design system

All roles share the same visual system.

## Rule 04 — No role-specific color themes

Role identity is communicated through content and navigation.

## Rule 05 — AI is native, not decorative

AI interfaces must communicate real state.

## Rule 06 — No fake AI progress

Never invent percentages or execution stages.

## Rule 07 — Long-running work is persistent

Browser navigation must not terminate server jobs.

## Rule 08 — SSE is transport

SSE does not become the source of truth.

## Rule 09 — Reconcile after events

Important events trigger server-state reconciliation.

## Rule 10 — Permission is backend-controlled

Frontend checks are UX protection, not security.

## Rule 11 — Search is authorization-aware

Never show inaccessible results.

## Rule 12 — Project Definition and Instance remain separate

No UI shortcut may collapse them.

## Rule 13 — Use route identity correctly

Resource identity belongs in routes.

## Rule 14 — Use query parameters correctly

Filtering/view state belongs in query parameters.

## Rule 15 — Avoid card overload

Grouping must have semantic value.

## Rule 16 — Avoid visual noise

Nothing should scream; hierarchy must still exist.

## Rule 17 — Accessibility is mandatory

Do not defer keyboard/semantic/accessibility work.

## Rule 18 — Responsive behavior is structural

Do not merely shrink desktop interfaces.

## Rule 19 — Errors are product states

Do not hide errors behind console logs.

## Rule 20 — Empty states are intentional

An empty list is not automatically an error.

## Rule 21 — Loading must preserve context

Avoid layout jumps when practical.

## Rule 22 — Mutation confirmation matters

Canonical mutations must reconcile with server state.

## Rule 23 — Sensitive information is minimized

Only show what the role and operation require.

## Rule 24 — No direct provider integration

All AI access goes through the backend architecture.

## Rule 25 — No frontend secrets

No provider/API secrets in client bundles.

## Rule 26 — No unnecessary global state

Keep state as local as possible.

## Rule 27 — Domain components should be reusable

Do not duplicate project/task/milestone/risk representations unnecessarily.

## Rule 28 — Observability must respect privacy

Do not log sensitive user or project content without justification.

## Rule 29 — Charts must answer questions

No decorative analytics.

## Rule 30 — Motion must have purpose

No animation for animation's sake.

## Rule 31 — Reduced motion must work

Functional meaning must survive reduced motion.

## Rule 32 — Deep links must be first-class

Pages should survive refresh and direct navigation.

## Rule 33 — Back navigation should be meaningful

Avoid workflows that destroy user context unnecessarily.

## Rule 34 — Preserve user input after recoverable errors

Do not make users retype everything.

## Rule 35 — Prevent accidental duplicate actions

Especially for:

- submissions;
- generation;
- destructive operations.

## Rule 36 — Confirm destructive actions

Use clear confirmation where consequences are meaningful.

## Rule 37 — Do not expose internal implementation unnecessarily

Users need meaningful system state, not internal framework details.

## Rule 38 — Keep public and workplace experiences distinct

Public can be expressive; authenticated workspaces prioritize productivity.

## Rule 39 — Use contextual imagery

Avoid generic stock-heavy product storytelling.

## Rule 40 — Maintain visual consistency

Typography, spacing, radii, iconography, status semantics, and surfaces must remain coherent.

